1) What openshift version have you worked on and how do you manage lifecycle ?
--> I have worked primarily on 4.x . Lifecycle management is handled throught redhat supported upgrade channel. I validate cluster operator health, deprecated APIs, nodes health, compatibility before upgrades .

2) Explain IPI vs UPI . When do you choose each ?
--> * IPI - Installer provision infrastructure . USed in less restricted environments.
    * UPI - User provisioned infrastructure. Infra is precreated . preferred in banking/ on-premises due to security, network control and compliance .
    In regulated environments UPI is mandatory .

3) How do you rollback if upgrade fails ?
--> Worker upgrades are paused immediately. If control-plan issue occurs, I restore from etcd backup, isolate faulty nodes and engage redhat support if required. Rollback planning start before upgrade execution.

4) How do SCC differ from Kubernetes PSPs?
--> SCC are more granular and enforce policies like 
      * runasnonroot
      * allowed volumes
      * SELINUX contexts
   Unlike PSPs(now removed), SCC are openshift native and enforced by default.

5) How do you design RBAC for multi team cluster ?
-->   * namespace level for developers
      * cluster roles only for platform teams
      * service accounts for CI/CD
      * no direct admin access . Admin access managed through PAG role .

6) How do you isolate traffic between applications?
--> Using network policies
      * default denies
      * Explicit allow rules

7) Explain openshift networking flow ?
-->   * OpenshiftSDN or OVN-kubernetes handles pod networking
      * Service handle east west traffic
      * route expose app externally
      * ingress controller terminates TLS
      Traffic is segmented and policy driven.

8) How do you handle persistent storage failure ?
-->   * Validate PVC/PV binding
      * Check CSI driver health
      * Validate backend storage
      * restore from snapshot/backup if required

9) What is your backup strategy ?
-->   * etcd backup for cluster health
      * Velero for namepaces and workloads

